  <p><strong>This is an academic and educational reference on peptide biochemistry.</strong> It is written for engineers, students, and researchers who want to understand receptor mechanisms, sequence homology, and evidence quality \u2014 the way a pharmacology review would present them.</p>
  <p><strong>It is not a vendor, formulation guide, or dosing reference.</strong> Nothing here describes how to synthesize, source, reconstitute, dose, stack, or administer any compound. The builder compares published mechanisms only; it does not and will not generate a recipe, ratio, or protocol.</p>
  <p><strong>Nothing here is medical advice.</strong> Several compounds described are unapproved for human use, prohibited in competitive sport, or available only as prescription pharmaceuticals. If you are taking or considering any of them, that conversation belongs with a licensed physician or pharmacist who knows your history \u2014 not with a web page.</p>
  <p><strong>Evidence quality varies enormously across these entries</strong>, which is why every one carries a grade. One compound here has been through large randomized trials. Others rest almost entirely on rodent studies from a small number of groups. Treating those as equivalent because both are called \u201cpeptides\u201d is the single most common error in this space.</p>
</div></div></section>`;
 
