function semaExtra(){
  return `
  <h3>If you are currently prescribed semaglutide</h3>
  <p>A note on why this section reads the way it does. Semaglutide is the one compound here that large numbers of people are actually taking, under prescription, right now. So the interaction question is not academic for them \u2014 and that is exactly why a web page is the wrong place to settle it.</p>
  <p>Here is what is genuinely documented in the prescribing information, and worth knowing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Delayed gastric emptying affects oral drug absorption.</strong> This is the mechanistically important one. Anything you swallow may be absorbed on a different timeline than it otherwise would. For most medications that is not clinically meaningful; for narrow-therapeutic-index drugs it can be.</li>
    <li><strong>Hypoglycemia risk rises sharply alongside insulin or sulfonylureas.</strong> Semaglutide's own insulin effect is glucose-dependent and carries low intrinsic hypoglycemia risk, but that protection does not extend to co-administered agents that lower glucose independent of glucose level. Dose adjustment of those agents is a standard clinical consideration.</li>
    <li><strong>Substantially reduced food intake has downstream nutritional consequences</strong> \u2014 protein intake, micronutrients, and hydration are routine points of clinical follow-up, not exotic concerns.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>And here is what is not documented, stated plainly: <strong>there are no clinical interaction studies between semaglutide and any research peptide catalogued on this site.</strong> Not one. The interaction table below reasons from receptor mechanism, which is a legitimate way to generate a hypothesis and a poor way to make a decision about your own body.</p>
  <p>The one mechanistic tension worth naming, because it is the plausible one and you will meet the question eventually: growth hormone signaling tends to increase insulin resistance, while GLP-1 receptor agonism improves glycemic control. Those push in opposite directions in principle. Whether that matters in practice, at what magnitude, for a specific person on a specific dose, is unstudied \u2014 and the honest answer to \u201cwhat happens if I combine these\u201d is that nobody has measured it.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <h4>Where this question actually belongs</h4>
    <p>Your prescriber and pharmacist have two things this page structurally cannot: your labs and history, and access to a full interaction database checked against everything else you take. If you are weighing anything alongside a GLP-1 agonist, that is a ten-minute conversation with someone qualified rather than a research project. Pharmacists in particular are underused for exactly this and are usually reachable without an appointment.</p>
  </div>`;
}
 
